/*!
    \externalpage ../theme_tutorial_select.html#theme_tutorial_select_entry_types
    \title Song selection entry types
*/

/*!
    \qmlmodule RhythmGameQml
    \title RhythmGame QML types
    \brief Provides game data and optional behavior for themes.

    Import RhythmGameQml to use the game's types. A theme chooses the Standard
    components it needs for each screen and supplies its own appearance.
    Start with a complete component such as StandardSelectController or
    StandardGameplayFlow, then use smaller components when you need custom behavior.
*/

/*!
    \page qml-api.html
    \title QML API reference

    Use the \l {../theme_tutorial.html}{theme tutorial} for a sequence of
    installable examples. The type pages describe the properties and methods
    available when you want to change those examples.

    \section1 Choose a starting component

    \table
        \header \li Screen \li Component \li What the theme draws
        \row \li Main menu \li \l StandardMainController \li Buttons, labels and images
        \row \li Multiplayer \li \l StandardMultiplayerFlow \li Room list and room controls
        \row \li Song selection \li \l StandardSelectController \li List or wheel and focus movement
        \row \li Decide \li \l StandardDecideFlow \li Chart or course details
        \row \li Gameplay \li \l StandardGameplayFlow \li Notes, BGA and scores
        \row \li Results \li \l StandardResultInput \li Chart results or a course summary
    \endtable

    Each complete component includes the helpers it needs. Don't add those
    helpers separately to the same screen, because they can handle input twice.

    \section1 Receive screen data

    Decide and gameplay declare \c {required property GameplayContext gameplay}
    on their root object. Bind the supplied value to the standard flow's
    \c gameplay property. Every gameplay theme must support courses, and the
    context supplies the same current-chart interface for a single chart or course.

    Normal results declare \c {required property ResultContext result}.
    Course summaries declare \c {required property CourseResultContext result}.
    Both can pass the value to StandardResultInput::result. The roles remain
    separate, even when a theme shares some display components between them.

    The game owns the contexts and their data. See \l GameplayContext,
    \l ResultContext and \l CourseResultContext for their properties and player types.

    \section1 Change behavior

    An action property documented as a replacement handles the whole operation.
    The standard action does not run afterward, and the return value is ignored.
    A \c try...Action callback runs before the standard action. Return true
    when it handled the request, or false or undefined to continue.

    Signals such as StandardGameplayFlow::closing let the theme respond without
    replacing an operation. Request signals ask the theme to provide UI or handle
    an action. They do not report success.

    \section1 Build a custom selector

    Use StandardSelectController for the common case. For custom input, start
    with StandardSelectState and supply your own controls. For custom browsing
    behavior, use the smaller components listed below.

    \table
        \header \li Component \li Responsibility
        \row \li \l StandardSelectState \li Browsing, focus, sorting, filtering and activation
        \row \li \l StandardSelectSession \li Folder loading, history storage and cached result lists
        \row \li \l StandardSelectActions \li Chart and course activation, saved results and leaving selection
        \row \li \l StandardSelectModelAdapter \li Repeated entries for a circular view
        \row \li \l StandardSelectNavigation \li Directional keys and scratch movement
        \row \li \l StandardSelectInput \li Navigation plus activation, replay, autoplay and back actions
        \row \li \l StandardSelectShortcuts \li Selection's F2, F3, F11 and F12 actions
        \row \li \l StandardSelectReload \li Table reload and root folder scans
        \row \li \l PendingReplyGroup \li Cancellation for a group of asynchronous replies
    \endtable

    \section1 Load saved score details

    Selection's results map contains BmsResult and BmsResultCourse objects from
    the profile's memory cache. These results contain points and settings,
    without recordings or gauge histories. Profile::scoreDb also exposes
    chartResults and courseResults maps for other screens. Bind to a chart MD5
    or course identifier in the appropriate map. Saves and imports update these
    bindings automatically. Result objects keep their identity for the lifetime
    of the profile, and imported changes update their properties in place.

    \l ScoreDetails takes a result and profile and loads one full score.
    Bind enabled to the screen or panel that needs it. The component cancels
    obsolete requests and exposes score, status and loading. Course scores
    retain combined events and gauges as well as their individual stage scores.
    Themes can calculate statistics in JavaScript from the loaded events.

    \section1 Add online rankings

    StandardSelectController includes \l StandardRanking as its \c ranking
    property. It follows the focused chart and profile. The controller's
    \c openInternetRanking() method and F11 open the selected provider's website.

    Use StandardRanking directly on results or other screens. Supply the chart
    and profile, and a score when showing a result. Themes draw the entries and
    positions; the shared component handles queries, provider settings, local
    LR2IR scores and website links. Course summaries have no online ranking.

    \section1 Play menu music and previews

    \l StandardMainController includes main-menu actions and background music.
    StandardSelectController includes \l StandardSelectPreview as \c preview
    and an AudioPlayer as \c backgroundMusic. The music stops while a preview
    plays. Both controllers stop their music when disabled.

    Customize the players' properties, or set either property to null to omit
    that player. Bind \c preview.moving to a list's movement state. Set
    \c preview.chartAudioEnabled to false to use preview files only.

    For a custom selector, StandardSelectPreview also works on its own with a
    \c selectState, or explicit \c chartData and \c previewSource properties.
    \l StandardMainActions provides menu actions without music.

    \section1 Show score targets

    \l StandardGameplayTarget takes a gameplay context and player index. It
    loads saved scores for each stage and supplies the profile's chosen target,
    including recorded progress, battle opponents and Arena targets. Themes
    draw the points and personal-best values. The helper is optional.

    \section1 Build a custom gameplay flow

    StandardGameplayFlow supplies startup, exit, retry and course progression.
    Its presentation timing properties let an animation delay startup or
    results without replacing score saving or navigation.

    \l StandardGameplayInput is available when a theme implements the rest of
    the flow itself. It maps Escape to StandardGameplayInput::exitRequested
    and handles the Start+Select retry gesture. The custom flow owns startup,
    score saving, results and course progression. Don't combine it with
    StandardGameplayFlow.

    \l StandardChartRetry supplies retry without choosing an input gesture.
    StandardGameplayFlow and StandardResultInput already expose
    \c retryAvailable and \c retry(samePattern) for theme buttons.
    \l StandardGameplayAttemptState tracks whether a player made a scoring hit.
    \l StandardInputKeys identifies bound lane keys for custom input handlers.

    \section1 Add Arena presentation

    A screen adds its own Arena panel. Use \l StandardArenaSelectOverlay for
    room controls, the ready shortcut and chat, \l StandardArenaGameplayOverlay
    for live standings, and \l StandardArenaResultOverlay for completed standings. Gameplay and
    result overlays take the same typed contexts as the rest of their screens.
    Course summaries do not need an Arena panel.

    These components share \l StandardArenaOverlay for saved placement and
    chat input. Replace its \c panelComponent to supply different visuals,
    or change \c chatShortcut to use another key. ContentFrame does not create
    a panel or call presentation methods on the screen.
*/
